\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Refining an admissible scalar field}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On a rectangular two-dimensional torus of periods #{N_1,N_2\ge 3}, let charge-one scalar links have oriented principal angles #{|\phi_p|<\theta\le\pi}. For every positive integer #{k} there is a fine scalar field #{RU} with the same coarse parallel transports along subdivided edges and with angle #{\phi_p/k^2} on each of the #{k^2} fine faces of #{p}. These conditions specify a single fine gauge class for each coarse gauge class. In particular #{w(RU)=w(U)} for the \vocabk{scalar line flux}{fdip-015V}.}

\proof{\p{On each positively oriented coarse edge assign fine transports #{1} to the first #{k-1} subedges and its old transport to the last; use inverse values for reversed edges. Inside a coarse square prescribe #{z_p=\ue^{\ui\phi_p/k^2}} for each small plaquette phase. Their product is its prescribed boundary product. Choose a spanning tree of the dual graph of its fine faces, using only interior primal edges to connect them. Assign #{1} to interior edges outside that tree. At a leaf all edges except the primal edge towards the parent are determined. Solve for its phase by multiplication and inversion and delete the leaf. At the root its remaining face constraint follows from the total prescribed product, since in the product of all faces the internal edges cancel. For #{k=1} the boundary already supplies its single face. Different cells share only the prescribed boundary edges.}

\p{The fine principal angles are exactly #{\phi_p/k^2}, which are strictly between #{-\pi} and #{\pi}. The contribution from one coarse square is #{\phi_p/(2\pi)}, and summing all square contributions over the closed torus gives its integer flux. Two such fine fields for the same coarse field have flat quotient with trivial coordinate-cycle holonomies: the products of their transports along each subdivided coarse edge agree. By the flat-field argument of \ref{fdip-01A4} the quotient is a site gauge, which can be normalized to equal #{1} at a coarse basepoint. Transport along the coarse skeleton then gives #{1} at every coarse vertex. For gauge-equivalent coarse fields extend the coarse site gauge arbitrarily to fine vertices. Multiplication along a fine path leaves only its endpoint gauges, so the same uniqueness argument applies.}}
